 # Business Rules — PegaFila
 
-Domain document for **PegaFilaWeb** — repository **frontend, backend and totem API** (Laravel monolith). Derived from product requirements. Core domain issuance is implemented via `App\Actions\IssueTicket` (ticket into an active queue) and deterministic call-next via `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (three call modes + 3-ticket fairness); HTTP, Livewire, and billing remain future work.
+Domain document for **PegaFilaWeb** — repository **frontend, backend and totem API** (Laravel monolith). Derived from product requirements. Core domain issuance is implemented via `App\Actions\IssueTicket` (ticket into an active queue) and deterministic call-next via `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (three call modes + 3-ticket fairness); queue lifecycle (`CreateQueue`, `UpdateQueue`), per-queue access grants (`SetQueueAccess`) and the `QueuePolicy` that decides who may call are implemented too; HTTP, Livewire, and billing remain future work.
 
 ## MVP Scope
 
@@ -98,7 +98,34 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Immutability enforcement:** the write-once rule is enforced on the `Ticket` model (`updating` hook), so it applies to Eloquent saves only — mass updates through the query builder (`Ticket::query()->update(...)`) bypass it and must not be used on issued tickets.
 
-**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields. Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); AuthZ stays out of scope per #20 and HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
+**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields. It locks the queue, rejects inactive queues (`InactiveQueueException`), authorizes the caller with the `call` ability of `QueuePolicy` (`AuthorizationException`, even if the ticket was already called), and only then rejects non-waiting tickets (`LogicException`). Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it); after locking the queue and checking it is active it authorizes the caller with the same `call` ability, in every mode. HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
+
+### Queue management
+
+Queues are managed through domain actions (no UI yet). Every action authorizes through `App\Policies\QueuePolicy` and writes nothing when denied (`AuthorizationException`).
+
+| Action | Who can | Rules |
+| ------ | ------- | ----- |
+| `CreateQueue` | Owner, admin of the entity | Name is trimmed, 1–255 chars. Slug defaults to `Str::slug(name)` (`Atendimento Geral` → `atendimento-geral`); an explicit slug is slugified too and must not end up empty. New queues are active. |
+| `UpdateQueue` | Owner, admin of the queue's entity | Any combination of name, slug and active flag; `null` leaves a field unchanged. Keeping the queue's own slug is fine. |
+
+- Slugs are unique **within an entity** (the same slug may exist in another entity). A duplicate — including a lost race on the DB unique index — is a `ValidationException`.
+- **No hard delete.** A queue is retired by deactivating it (`is_active = false`): its tickets are kept, and `IssueTicket` / `CallNextTicket` / `Ticket::call()` keep failing with `InactiveQueueException`. There is no delete/remove/destroy queue action by design.
+- Employees, members of other entities and non-members cannot create or update queues.
+
+### Queue access (who may call)
+
+An employee may call tickets on a queue only through an explicit grant in `queue_access` (one row per membership and queue, `permissions` = JSON array of strings). The only permission today is `call` (`App\Enums\QueuePermission`).
+
+`App\Actions\SetQueueAccess` (owner/admin of the queue's entity only — an employee cannot grant themselves access) sets the permissions of a membership on a queue: a non-empty list upserts the row, an empty list revokes (deletes) it. Unknown or non-string entries, or a membership and queue of different entities, are a `ValidationException` and nothing is stored. Grants are recorded on the `audit` log channel as `queue.access.granted` / `queue.access.revoked` with `actor_id`, `queue_id` and `entity_membership_id`; a revoke with no row to delete is not logged.
+
+**`QueuePolicy` (single source of truth):**
+
+| Ability | Allowed |
+| ------- | ------- |
+| `create` (argument: the entity) | Owner/admin of that entity |
+| `update`, `manageAccess` | Owner/admin of the queue's entity |
+| `call` | Owner/admin of the queue's entity always (no grant row needed); an employee only with a `queue_access` row for **that** queue whose `permissions` is an array containing `call` (a corrupted non-array value denies); everyone else denied |
 
 ### Priority
 
